\description{
Demand for several products often rises and falls together in the same
period, so resampling each product separately can misstate joint
stockout risk. \code{scenario_replenish()} builds \emph{joint} demand scenarios
for several stock-keeping units (SKUs) and chooses a replenishment
plan that globally minimizes the empirical conditional value-at-risk
(CVaR) of stockout plus holding loss over integer plans.

The procedure:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Align histories by period.} Every SKU's history is restricted to
the periods shared by all SKUs, so that each scenario resamples the
same periods for every SKU.
\item \strong{Draw joint bootstrap scenarios.} For each scenario, one vector
of period indices is drawn with replacement and applied to every
SKU, preserving cross-SKU co-movement.
\item \strong{Fit and predict natively.} In each scenario, each SKU's
resampled history is fitted with the same Gaussian engine used by
\code{\link[=fit.infer]{fit.infer()}} (\code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} with \code{family = "gaussian"}), and
demand is predicted from that SKU's own future features. Negative
predictions are truncated to zero. No precomputed demand is ever
substituted for these fits and predictions.
\item \strong{Optimize exactly.} The empirical CVaR of total stockout plus
holding loss is minimized globally over all integer replenishment
plans respecting each SKU's \code{capacity} and the total \code{budget}. The
CVaR retains the fractional weight of scenarios sitting exactly at
the tail boundary rather than averaging a top-\code{ceil()} count of
scenario losses.
}

If any step fails---invalid input, a failed or rank-deficient fit, a
non-finite prediction, or a candidate space larger than
\code{max_combinations}---the function errors and returns nothing rather
than delivering a partial result.
}
\details{
\code{unit_cost}, \code{holding_cost}, \code{stockout_cost}, and \code{capacity} may each
be a single number recycled to every SKU or a named numeric vector
with one element per SKU.
}
\section{Scope}{

All reported risk quantities are \emph{empirical} quantities of the
regression-bootstrap scenarios resampled from the aligned history.
They describe resampling risk only; they are not a guarantee of future
demand coverage or of population-level risk.
}

\section{Reproducibility}{
When using the infer package for research, or in other cases when exact
reproducibility is a priority, be sure the set the seed for R’s random
number generator. infer will respect the random seed specified in the
\code{set.seed()} function, returning the same result when \code{generate()}ing
data given an identical seed. For instance, we can calculate the
difference in mean \code{age} by \code{college} degree status using the \code{gss}
dataset from 10 versions of the \code{gss} resampled with permutation using
the following code.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{set.seed(1)

gss |>
  specify(age ~ college) |>
  hypothesize(null = "independence") |>
  generate(reps = 5, type = "permute") |>
  calculate("diff in means", order = c("degree", "no degree"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{## Response: age (numeric)
## Explanatory: college (factor)
## Null Hypothesis: indepe...
## # A tibble: 5 x 2
##   replicate   stat
##       <int>  <dbl>
## 1         1 -0.531
## 2         2 -2.35
## 3         3  0.764
## 4         4  0.280
## 5         5  0.350
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Setting the seed to the same value again and rerunning the same code
will produce the same result.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# set the seed
set.seed(1)

gss |>
  specify(age ~ college) |>
  hypothesize(null = "independence") |>
  generate(reps = 5, type = "permute") |>
  calculate("diff in means", order = c("degree", "no degree"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{## Response: age (numeric)
## Explanatory: college (factor)
## Null Hypothesis: indepe...
## # A tibble: 5 x 2
##   replicate   stat
##       <int>  <dbl>
## 1         1 -0.531
## 2         2 -2.35
## 3         3  0.764
## 4         4  0.280
## 5         5  0.350
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Please keep this in mind when writing infer code that utilizes
resampling with \code{generate()}.
}

\examples{
set.seed(1)

# two SKUs whose demands move together period by period
history <- expand.grid(period = 1:24, sku = c("a", "b"))
history$demand <- ifelse(history$sku == "a", 20, 35) +
  2 * history$period +
  rep(rnorm(24, sd = 3), times = 2) +
  rnorm(48, sd = 1)

future <- data.frame(sku = c("a", "b"), period = 25)

scenario_replenish(
  history,
  demand ~ period,
  future,
  sku = sku,
  period = period,
  reps = 100,
  unit_cost = 2,
  holding_cost = 1,
  stockout_cost = 8,
  capacity = 200,
  budget = 800,
  alpha = 0.9
)

}
